1
00:00:00,134 --> 00:00:03,093
Hello, and a very warm welcome to Shah Spectrum.

2
00:00:03,093 --> 00:00:05,056
If you're new here, welcome.

3
00:00:05,056 --> 00:00:10,085
If you're back again, it's lovely to have you with us once more.

4
00:00:10,729 --> 00:00:15,189
Think of the emergency fund a family keeps for a rainy day.

5
00:00:15,189 --> 00:00:18,162
Now picture one for a whole country.

6
00:00:18,162 --> 00:00:22,869
India's has just shrunk by more than eight billion dollars in a single week.

7
00:00:22,869 --> 00:00:23,847
Should that worry us?

8
00:00:24,526 --> 00:00:29,338
I'm Mittal Shah, or rather an AI-generated version of my voice, and I'll be taking

9
00:00:29,338 --> 00:00:30,511
you through today's story.

10
00:00:31,551 --> 00:00:33,058
First, what happened.

11
00:00:33,058 --> 00:00:39,774
Every Friday, the Reserve Bank of India, the R-B-I, publishes how much foreign money

12
00:00:39,774 --> 00:00:41,267
the country is holding.

13
00:00:41,267 --> 00:00:47,064
In the week ending the ninth of October, India's foreign exchange reserves fell by

14
00:00:47,064 --> 00:00:49,020
eight point five eight billion dollars.

15
00:00:49,020 --> 00:00:53,151
They now stand at just over seven hundred and twenty-six billion dollars.

16
00:00:53,151 --> 00:00:58,217
That is according to the Reserve Bank's weekly data, released on the sixteenth of

17
00:00:58,217 --> 00:00:58,886
October.

18
00:00:58,886 --> 00:01:00,671
And it was not a one-off.

19
00:01:00,671 --> 00:01:03,348
It was the latest in a run of weekly falls.

20
00:01:03,928 --> 00:01:06,672
Now, what exactly are these reserves?

21
00:01:06,672 --> 00:01:10,978
Think of them as the country's emergency fund, held in foreign currency.

22
00:01:10,978 --> 00:01:15,796
The Reserve Bank keeps them to pay for imports, to repay foreign loans if needed,

23
00:01:15,796 --> 00:01:18,473
and to steady the rupee when it swings sharply.

24
00:01:18,473 --> 00:01:23,225
Most of it is foreign currency assets, such as bonds and deposits held abroad.

25
00:01:23,225 --> 00:01:29,834
According to the Reserve Bank's data, they make up about four-fifths of the total.

26
00:01:29,834 --> 00:01:35,240
Most of the rest is gold, plus small holdings with the International Monetary Fund.

27
00:01:35,870 --> 00:01:37,809
So why do reserves go down?

28
00:01:37,809 --> 00:01:38,647
There are two main reasons.

29
00:01:38,647 --> 00:01:41,004
First, the Reserve Bank can sell dollars.

30
00:01:41,004 --> 00:01:46,298
When the rupee is weakening fast, the bank can sell some of its dollars into the

31
00:01:46,298 --> 00:01:46,898
market.

32
00:01:46,817 --> 00:01:49,673
That adds demand for rupees and slows the fall.

33
00:01:49,673 --> 00:01:50,614
Second, prices move.

34
00:01:50,614 --> 00:01:55,611
Gold and foreign bonds are valued in dollars every week, so the total can rise or

35
00:01:55,611 --> 00:01:58,890
fall even when nobody buys or sells anything.

36
00:01:58,890 --> 00:02:04,593
For example, if gold becomes cheaper in dollar terms, India's gold is worth less on

37
00:02:04,593 --> 00:02:08,441
paper, even though the Reserve Bank has not sold a single gram.

38
00:02:09,019 --> 00:02:12,800
The Reserve Bank's table does not say which reason applies this week.

39
00:02:12,800 --> 00:02:17,249
Market reports link the recent falls to the bank selling dollars to support the

40
00:02:17,249 --> 00:02:17,849
rupee.

41
00:02:17,775 --> 00:02:24,510
That is a reasonable explanation, but treat it as an inference, not a confirmed

42
00:02:24,510 --> 00:02:25,110
fact.

43
00:02:25,346 --> 00:02:27,121
Here is why that matters for India.

44
00:02:27,121 --> 00:02:29,683
A weaker rupee makes imports costlier.

45
00:02:29,683 --> 00:02:34,077
That includes petrol, electronics, and fees for studying abroad.

46
00:02:34,077 --> 00:02:39,895
If the Reserve Bank can slow a sharp slide in the rupee, it can soften that blow for

47
00:02:39,895 --> 00:02:40,644
households.

48
00:02:40,644 --> 00:02:43,156
And the reserves are what give it the room to do that.

49
00:02:43,156 --> 00:02:47,385
One likely effect of a large cushion is confidence:

50
00:02:47,385 --> 00:02:52,932
lenders and businesses abroad tend to feel more comfortable dealing with a country

51
00:02:52,932 --> 00:02:54,319
that can cover its bills.

52
00:02:54,319 --> 00:02:57,667
That is inference, not a number from the data.

53
00:02:58,073 --> 00:02:59,384
So, a quick recap:

54
00:02:59,384 --> 00:03:03,031
the reserves are a cushion, and the cushion has been used.

55
00:03:03,632 --> 00:03:05,695
But look at the size.

56
00:03:05,695 --> 00:03:11,575
Even after these falls, reserves are still about twenty-eight billion dollars higher

57
00:03:11,575 --> 00:03:15,035
than a year ago, according to the same data.

58
00:03:15,035 --> 00:03:19,583
This looks more like a cushion doing its job than a cushion running out.

59
00:03:19,583 --> 00:03:22,797
That is also inference, but it fits the numbers.

60
00:03:23,445 --> 00:03:24,812
What do we not know?

61
00:03:24,812 --> 00:03:29,660
We do not know how long the falls will continue, or exactly how much comes from

62
00:03:29,660 --> 00:03:32,391
dollar sales and how much from price changes.

63
00:03:32,391 --> 00:03:36,248
The next figure is due on Friday, the twenty-third of October.

64
00:03:36,248 --> 00:03:38,578
That will show whether the dip is slowing.

65
00:03:39,237 --> 00:03:40,142
To sum up:

66
00:03:40,142 --> 00:03:45,427
India's emergency fund has been shrinking for several weeks, but it is built to be

67
00:03:45,427 --> 00:03:47,395
used, and it is still large.

68
00:03:48,395 --> 00:03:50,416
A quick note before you go.

69
00:03:50,416 --> 00:03:53,045
This episode is meant to explain the news.

70
00:03:53,045 --> 00:03:56,049
It should not be considered investment advice.

71
00:03:56,598 --> 00:03:59,784
Thanks for listening, and see you in the next episode.

